  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) has policies in place to address unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. However, a review of DOD's policies and practices found that they do not consistently include key elements for preventing and responding to these behaviors.\n\nThe Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence includes six principles: risk factors, protective factors, primary prevention strategies, secondary prevention strategies, tertiary prevention strategies, and risk domains. DOD's policies on sexual harassment include some of these principles, but not all. Specifically, DOD's policies do not consistently include risk factors and protective factors, which are critical for understanding and addressing the root causes of unwanted sexual behaviors.\n\nThe review also found that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not consistently include mechanisms for anonymous reporting, which is required by law. While some military services have implemented anonymous reporting mechanisms, others have not. This inconsistency creates a risk that incidents of sexual harassment may not be reported, and that perpetrators may not be held accountable.\n\nIn addition, the review found that DOD's data collection and reporting practices for unwanted sexual behaviors are inconsistent. While DOD has reliable data systems for collecting and reporting data on sexual assault and domestic violence, the data collection and reporting practices for sexual harassment are inconsistent across the military services. This inconsistency makes it difficult for DOD to develop a comprehensive understanding of the scope and nature of unwanted sexual behaviors in the military.\n\nTo address these issues, the Government Accountability Office (GAO) recommends that DOD:\n\n1. Fully include the CDC principles in its new policy on sexual harassment.\n2. Include mechanisms for anonymous reporting in its new policy on sexual harassment.\n3. Develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents.\n4. Incorporate key elements of a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework into its continuum of harm prevention strategy.\n\nBy implementing these recommendations, DOD can improve its policies and practices for preventing and responding to unwanted sexual behaviors, and ensure that its data collection and reporting practices are consistent and reliable.",
